IMPORT daydate
IMPORT `wa-interpretation-time`

-- Interpretation Act 1984 (Western Australia) -- PART V: REPEAL OF WRITTEN LAW (ss 33 to 39).
-- What a repeal does to things done, to rights, duties and penalties, to proceedings, and to subsidiary legislation;
-- whether a repeal of a repealing law revives anything; when a repealed enactment stops operating.

§ `Part V -- Repeal of written law`

§§ `33. Repeal of written law includes repeal of amendments`

DECLARE `Provision of another written law` HAS
    `its citation`                                   IS A STRING
    `it amended the repealed written law`            IS A BOOLEAN

-- Section 33: where a written law that has been amended by any other written law is repealed, the repeal includes
-- the repeal of all those provisions of the other written law by which the first-mentioned law was amended.
-- Provisions of other laws that did not amend the repealed law are not included.
-- src:434 | Where a written law which has been amended by any other written law is repealed, such repeal shall include the repeal of all those provisions of such other written law by which the firstmentioned written law was amended.
@ref s 33 (src:434)
GIVEN provisions IS A LIST OF `Provision of another written law`
GIVETH A LIST OF STRING
`the provisions repealed along with a repealed written law` provisions MEANS
    map (GIVEN p YIELD p's `its citation`) (filter (GIVEN p YIELD p's `it amended the repealed written law`) provisions)

§§ `34. Repeal of repealing enactment, effect of`

-- Section 34: where a written law repeals a repealing enactment, the repeal does not revive any enactment
-- previously repealed unless words are added reviving it.
-- src:436 | Where a written law repeals a repealing enactment, the repeal does not revive any enactment previously repealed unless words are added reviving it.
@ref s 34 (src:436)
GIVEN `words are added reviving the enactment previously repealed` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`the repeal of a repealing enactment revives the enactment previously repealed` `words are added reviving the enactment previously repealed` MEANS
    `words are added reviving the enactment previously repealed`

§§ `35. Repeal and substitution of provision, effect of`

-- Section 35: where a written law repeals an enactment and substitutes provisions for it, the repealed enactment
-- remains in operation until the substituted provisions come into operation.  The substituted provisions come into
-- operation at the beginning of their commencement day (s 21), so on that day the repealed enactment no longer
-- operates.
-- src:438 | Where a written law repeals an enactment and substitutes provisions for the enactment repealed, the repealed enactment remains in operation until the substituted provisions come into operation.
@ref s 35 (src:438)
GIVEN d IS A DATE
      `the day the substituted provisions come into operation` IS A DATE
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`the repealed enactment remains in operation on` d `until the substituted provisions come into operation on` `the day the substituted provisions come into operation` MEANS
    Day d LESS THAN Day `the day the substituted provisions come into operation`

§§ `36. Repealing and reenacting a provision, effect of`

DECLARE `Thing existing under a repealed and reenacted provision` IS ONE OF
    `a district or other local division or area`
    `a council, corporation, board, tribunal, commission, trust or other body constituted, or an election or appointment of its members`
    `an office constituted or an appointment of an officer`
    `subsidiary legislation, a warrant, a certificate or a document made`
    `any other act, matter or thing`

-- Section 36: where a written law repeals and reenacts, with or without modification, any enactment, all of
-- the things listed in paragraphs (a) to (e) that are in existence, in force or in operation at the commencement of
-- the repealing law under or for the purposes of the provision, subsist and enure for the purposes of the repealing
-- law, in so far as is consistent with it, and continue as if the repealing law had been in operation when they
-- originated and had been constituted, made or done under it.  The five kinds make no difference to the answer.
-- src:440 | Where a written law repeals and reenacts, with or without modification, any enactment —
-- src:441 | (a) all districts or other local divisions or areas; and
-- src:442 | (b) all councils, corporations, boards, tribunals, commissions, trusts, or other bodies constituted, and all elections and appointments of members thereof made; and
-- src:443 | (c) all offices constituted and appointments of officers made; and
-- src:444 | (d) all subsidiary legislation, warrants, certificates, and documents made; and
-- src:445 | (e) all other acts, matters, and things whatsoever,
-- src:446 | which, at the commencement of the repealing law, are respectively in existence, or in force or operation, under or for the purposes of such provision, shall, in so far as is consistent with the repealing law, subsist and enure for the purposes of such law and shall continue as if the repealing law had been in operation when they respectively originated or were constituted, made or done and they had originated or been constituted, made or done under that law.
@ref s 36 (src:440-446)
GIVEN x IS A `Thing existing under a repealed and reenacted provision`
      `it was in existence, in force or in operation at the commencement of the repealing law, under or for the purposes of the repealed provision` IS A BOOLEAN
      `it is consistent with the repealing law` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`the thing subsists and continues as if made or done under the repealing law` x `it was in existence, in force or in operation at the commencement of the repealing law, under or for the purposes of the repealed provision` `it is consistent with the repealing law` MEANS
        `it was in existence, in force or in operation at the commencement of the repealing law, under or for the purposes of the repealed provision`
    AND `it is consistent with the repealing law`

§§ `37. General savings on repeal`

DECLARE `What a repeal might be said to affect` IS ONE OF
    `reviving anything not in force or existing at the time the repeal takes effect`
    `the previous operation of the repealed enactment, or anything duly done or suffered under it`
    `a right, interest, title, power or privilege created, acquired, accrued, established or exercisable, or a status or capacity existing, before the repeal`
    `a duty, obligation, liability or burden of proof imposed, created or incurred before the repeal`
    `a penalty or forfeiture incurred or liable to be incurred for an offence committed against the repealed enactment`
    `an investigation, legal proceeding or remedy in respect of any such right, interest, title, power, privilege, status, capacity, duty, obligation, liability, burden of proof, penalty or forfeiture`

-- Section 37(1): where a written law repeals an enactment the repeal does not, unless the contrary intention
-- appears, (a) revive anything not in force or existing when the repeal takes effect; (b) affect the previous
-- operation of the repealed enactment or anything duly done or suffered under it; (c) affect any right,
-- interest, title, power or privilege created, acquired, accrued, established or exercisable or any status or
-- capacity existing before the repeal; (d) affect any duty, obligation, liability or burden of proof imposed,
-- created or incurred before the repeal; (e) subject to s 11 of The Criminal Code and s 10 of the Sentencing Act
-- 1995, affect any penalty or forfeiture incurred or liable to be incurred for an offence committed against the
-- enactment; or (f) affect any investigation, legal proceeding or remedy in respect of any of those.
-- JUST FALSE is "the repeal does not affect it".  Where the contrary intention appears, or (for a penalty) the
-- Criminal Code or Sentencing Act section applies, the answer lies in a text that is not among the sources: NOTHING.
-- src:448 | (1) Where a written law repeals an enactment, the repeal does not, unless the contrary intention appears —
-- src:449 | (a) revive anything not in force or existing at the time at which the repeal takes effect;
-- src:450 | (b) affect the previous operation of the enactment repealed or anything duly done or suffered under that enactment;
-- src:451 | (c) affect any right, interest, title, power or privilege created, acquired, accrued, established or exercisable or any status or capacity existing prior to the repeal;
-- src:452 | (d) affect any duty, obligation, liability, or burden of proof imposed, created, or incurred prior to the repeal;
-- src:453 | (e) subject to section 11 of The Criminal Code and section 10 of the Sentencing Act 1995, affect any penalty or forfeiture incurred or liable to be incurred in respect of an offence committed against that enactment;
-- src:454 | (f) affect any investigation, legal proceeding or remedy in respect of any such right, interest, title, power, privilege, status, capacity, duty, obligation, liability, burden of proof, penalty or forfeiture,
@ref s 37(1) (src:448)
@ref s 37(1)(a) (src:449)
@ref s 37(1)(b) (src:450)
@ref s 37(1)(c) (src:451)
@ref s 37(1)(d) (src:452)
@ref s 37(1)(e) (src:453)
@ref s 37(1)(f) (src:454)
GIVEN x IS A `What a repeal might be said to affect`
      `the contrary intention appears` IS A BOOLEAN
      `section 11 of The Criminal Code or section 10 of the Sentencing Act 1995 applies to the penalty or forfeiture` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`the repeal affects` x `the contrary intention appears` `section 11 of The Criminal Code or section 10 of the Sentencing Act 1995 applies to the penalty or forfeiture` MEANS
    IF `the contrary intention appears`
    THEN NOTHING
    ELSE
        CONSIDER x
        WHEN `a penalty or forfeiture incurred or liable to be incurred for an offence committed against the repealed enactment` THEN
            IF `section 11 of The Criminal Code or section 10 of the Sentencing Act 1995 applies to the penalty or forfeiture` THEN NOTHING ELSE JUST FALSE
        OTHERWISE JUST FALSE

-- Section 37(1), closing words: any such investigation, legal proceeding or remedy may be instituted, continued
-- or enforced, and any such penalty or forfeiture may be imposed and enforced, as if the repealing written law had
-- not been passed or made.  It follows the same two gates as the paragraphs it closes.
-- src:455 | and any such investigation, legal proceeding or remedy may be instituted, continued, or enforced, and any such penalty or forfeiture may be imposed and enforced as if the repealing written law had not been passed or made.
@ref s 37(1) (src:455)
GIVEN x IS A `What a repeal might be said to affect`
      `the contrary intention appears` IS A BOOLEAN
      `section 11 of The Criminal Code or section 10 of the Sentencing Act 1995 applies to the penalty or forfeiture` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`it may still be instituted, continued, enforced or imposed as if the repealing law had not been passed or made` x `the contrary intention appears` `section 11 of The Criminal Code or section 10 of the Sentencing Act 1995 applies to the penalty or forfeiture` MEANS
    CONSIDER x
    WHEN `a penalty or forfeiture incurred or liable to be incurred for an offence committed against the repealed enactment` THEN
        CONSIDER `the repeal affects` x `the contrary intention appears` `section 11 of The Criminal Code or section 10 of the Sentencing Act 1995 applies to the penalty or forfeiture`
        WHEN JUST affected THEN JUST (NOT affected)
        WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING
    WHEN `an investigation, legal proceeding or remedy in respect of any such right, interest, title, power, privilege, status, capacity, duty, obligation, liability, burden of proof, penalty or forfeiture` THEN
        CONSIDER `the repeal affects` x `the contrary intention appears` `section 11 of The Criminal Code or section 10 of the Sentencing Act 1995 applies to the penalty or forfeiture`
        WHEN JUST affected THEN JUST (NOT affected)
        WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING
    OTHERWISE NOTHING

-- Section 37(2): an express saving in the repealing provisions does not prejudice the operation of s 37.
-- src:456 | (2) The inclusion in the repealing provisions of an enactment of any express saving with respect to the repeals effected thereby shall not be taken to prejudice the operation of this section with respect to the effect of those repeals.
@ref s 37(2) (src:456)
GIVEN `the repealing provisions include an express saving` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`section 37 still operates` `the repealing provisions include an express saving` MEANS TRUE

§§ `38. Repeal of Act, effect of on subsidiary legislation`

-- Section 38(1): where an Act (a) repeals an Act and substitutes other provisions for it, or (b) repeals and
-- reenacts an Act, with or without modification, any subsidiary legislation made under the repealed Act and in
-- operation immediately before the commencement of the repealing Act continues in operation and has effect for all
-- purposes as if made under the repealing Act, so far as it is consistent with it.
-- src:459 | (1) Where an Act —
-- src:460 | (a) repeals an Act and substitutes other provisions therefor; or
-- src:461 | (b) repeals and reenacts an Act, with or without modification,
-- src:462 | any subsidiary legislation made under the repealed Act and in operation immediately before the commencement of the repealing Act shall, so far as it is consistent with the repealing Act, continue in operation and have effect for all purposes as if made under the repealing Act.
@ref s 38(1) (src:459-462)
GIVEN `the Act repeals an Act and substitutes other provisions, or repeals and reenacts it` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the subsidiary legislation was in operation immediately before the commencement of the repealing Act` IS A BOOLEAN
      `it is consistent with the repealing Act` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`the subsidiary legislation continues in operation as if made under the repealing Act` `the Act repeals an Act and substitutes other provisions, or repeals and reenacts it` `the subsidiary legislation was in operation immediately before the commencement of the repealing Act` `it is consistent with the repealing Act` MEANS
        `the Act repeals an Act and substitutes other provisions, or repeals and reenacts it`
    AND `the subsidiary legislation was in operation immediately before the commencement of the repealing Act`
    AND `it is consistent with the repealing Act`

-- Section 38(2): subsidiary legislation that continues in operation under s 38(1) may be amended or repealed as if
-- it had been made under the repealing Act.
-- src:463 | (2) Subsidiary legislation which continues in operation under subsection (1) may be amended or repealed as if it had been made under the repealing Act.
@ref s 38(2) (src:463)
GIVEN `the subsidiary legislation continues in operation under subsection (1)` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`the continuing subsidiary legislation may be amended or repealed as if made under the repealing Act` `the subsidiary legislation continues in operation under subsection (1)` MEANS
    `the subsidiary legislation continues in operation under subsection (1)`

§§ `39. Expiry of enactment, effect of`

-- Section 39: upon the expiry or lapse of any enactment, s 37 applies as if the enactment had been repealed.
-- src:465 | Upon the expiry or lapse of any enactment, the provisions of section 37 apply as if that enactment had been repealed.
@ref s 39 (src:465)
GIVEN x IS A `What a repeal might be said to affect`
      `the contrary intention appears` IS A BOOLEAN
      `section 11 of The Criminal Code or section 10 of the Sentencing Act 1995 applies to the penalty or forfeiture` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`the expiry or lapse of an enactment affects` x `the contrary intention appears` `section 11 of The Criminal Code or section 10 of the Sentencing Act 1995 applies to the penalty or forfeiture` MEANS
    `the repeal affects` x `the contrary intention appears` `section 11 of The Criminal Code or section 10 of the Sentencing Act 1995 applies to the penalty or forfeiture`
